mic press signal: Indiana University Press / Indiana University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.28,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Indiana University Press / Indiana University Press"]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"demonstrates\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Minsk","confidence":0.95}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Sovietization in Minsk, despite violent social changes under communism, preserved continuities with prerevolutionary Jewish life, with many Jews acculturating to Soviet society while retaining older patterns of identity.","confidence":0.72},"academicOrientation":{"score":70,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.78,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"8a8f13e8236c38f5a96a16f00dd4683b1d08c5e689c57b16e71ca23f1c09de9f","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"Minsk, the present capital of Belarus, was a heavily Jewish city in the decades between the world wars. Recasting our understanding of Soviet Jewish history, Becoming Soviet Jews demonstrates that the often violent social changes enforced by the communist project did not destroy continuities with prerevolutionary forms of Jewish life in Minsk. Using Minsk as a case study of the Sovietization of Jews in the former Pale of Settlement, Elissa Bemporad reveals the ways in which many Jews acculturated to Soviet society in the 1920s and 1930s while remaining committed to older patterns of Jewish identity, such as Yiddish culture and education, attachment to the traditions of the Jewish workers' Bund, circumcision, and kosher slaughter. This pioneering study also illuminates the reshaping of gender relations on the Jewish street and explores Jewish everyday life and identity during the years of the Great Terror.","pageCount":276,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"g